-Two approaches remain credible:
+A visit is considered unresolved when it has two or more pending revisions that share a common base or are otherwise in conflict. Unresolved visits are excluded from export eligibility until resolved.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Concurrency Policy
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Whole-visit revisions keep each submitted visit intact and send competing revisions to review. Each edit names its starting revision via baseRevisionId. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. No automatic merging is performed; the worker's submitted visit remains intact as a unit.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Trade-off: Even edits to unrelated fields create review work, increasing the review queue size. The benefit is preservation of whole-visit intent and avoidance of merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. The server does not need to understand relationships between species identification and cover estimates, observer lists, or notes.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The tablets must track base revision IDs locally and surface when a worker's draft is based on an outdated revision. Review-export must present a revision comparison screen and hold unresolved visits out of the CSV until a coordinator selects a winning revision.
+
+## Resolution Operations
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit by selecting one pending revision as the new resolved revision. The operation records coordinatorId, resolution timestamp, selected revisionId, and the set of contributing revision IDs that were superseded. The original submissions are retained; resolution does not delete competing revisions. A resolved revision can later acquire new competing revisions if further edits arrive based on it.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit handling | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visits as units and avoids merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Field-aware merge would reduce review volume by automatically combining disjoint field changes and only review overlapping changes, getting more visits ready for export. It was considered because it reduces coordinator workload and submission size. Rejected because it requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and automatically combined fields can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. User prefers larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. |
+| Revision identity | Server-generated revisionId per submission, client baseRevisionId reference | Client-generated revision IDs | Server control ensures uniqueness and auditability; base reference enables detection of concurrent edits. Client-generated IDs would shift uniqueness burden to tablets and complicate conflict detection. |
+
+## Downstream Accommodations
+
+Tablet-sync
+- Must store baseRevisionId alongside each local draft and the last known server revisionId for the visit.
+- On submit, include baseRevisionId in the envelope. On acceptance, update local base to the returned revisionId.
+- On retrieval, if pendingRevisions exist for a visit the worker is editing, surface a warning that a competing revision exists and offer to view/compare.
+- Retry state machine must preserve the same idempotencyKey and baseRevisionId; a change in base requires a new edit.
+
+Review-export
+- Must query record-store current-state view and filter out visits where pendingRevisions is non-empty.
+- Must present a revision comparison screen for coordinators showing two or more pending revisions side-by-side with author, submittedAt, baseRevisionId, and full payload diff.
+- Export eligibility rule: a visit is eligible only when resolvedRevisionId is non-null and pendingRevisions is empty.
+- Must retain provenance in export metadata: resolved revisionId, resolution timestamp, coordinatorId, and superseded revision IDs for auditability.
